===== docs/plans/261006-1010-csat-rating/plan.md =====
# CSAT rating — implementation plan

Spec: `docs/specs/csat-rating.md` (approved, Mai Do, 2026-01-12).

## Goal

A customer rates a resolved ticket 1–5 from the portal, once; a low score (1–2) emails the
owning agent; a nightly job writes each agent's rating count and average over the past seven
days; the `assignee_id` field becomes `owner_id` everywhere.

## Phases

| # | Phase | Status | Gate | Blocked by |
|---|-------|--------|------|------------|
| 1 | [Rename `assignee_id` → `owner_id`](phase-01-rename-owner-id.md) | not started | ACT (see Q1) | none |
| 2 | [Rate a resolved ticket](phase-02-rating.md) | not started | ACT, with a COUNCIL note (see below) | 1 |
| 3 | [Nightly weekly rating summary](phase-03-weekly-summary.md) | not started | ACT | 2 |

Phase 1 is a prefactoring phase: it changes a field name, not behaviour, and every existing test
is rewritten to the new name before the source changes. Phase 2 is the thinnest end-to-end slice
of the feature (store → portal → notify). Phase 3 widens it with the reporting job the spec asks
for, which can only be written once ratings exist to summarise.

## COUNCIL note on Phase 2

This repo's `store.js` is in-memory; the README says production puts the same interface over
Postgres. Adding `rating_score` and `rated_at` to the ticket row therefore implies a real column
addition in production that this repo cannot perform or verify. Phase 2 is safe to build and test
here, but whoever owns the production schema should confirm the migration before this ships
there — that confirmation is not a step in this plan.

## External reader note (Phase 1)

